<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- agent-infra-plist-version: 0.1.0 -->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- com.eldato.disk-reaper: the Mac's clock for the CONSOLIDATED disk reaper (#2048).

         ONE PASS, TWO SCHEDULERS. This job runs `disk-reaper.sh`, the same entry point with the same
         mode flag that the cells run from their systemd timer
         (templates/systemd/eldato-disk-reaper.service). Before this job existed the Mac had FOUR
         triggers (com.eldato.pi-cache-reap, com.tortoise.worktree-reaper, com.tortoise.branch-reaper,
         com.eldato.pi-session-reaper), four report formats, and no single answer to "is this box being
         reaped, how much did the last pass free, did it succeed". Two of the four reaped a class the
         consolidated pass owns, so BOTH ARE RETIRED (templates/launchd/RETIRED) and replaced by THIS
         job: com.eldato.pi-cache-reap becomes the `uv` class, com.tortoise.worktree-reaper becomes the
         `worktree` class. The two that remain are NOT among the three disk sinks and nothing here
         duplicates them: com.tortoise.branch-reaper deletes local BRANCH REFS (a class no sink pass
         owns) and com.eldato.pi-session-reaper reaps idle pi PROCESSES.

         THIS TEMPLATE IS `.plist.tmpl` ON PURPOSE, AND THAT IS THE OWNERSHIP MARKER.
         scripts/install-launchd.sh renders `templates/launchd/*.plist`; this one is rendered ONLY by
         scripts/install-disk-reaper-schedule.sh, because it needs machine state install-launchd does
         not carry (the pass path, the mode flag, the state dir, the repos) and because a job that takes
         an ARMING decision must have exactly one installer able to take it. install-launchd.sh does not
         match `*.plist.tmpl`, so it cannot render this job half-empty and bootstrap a job whose
         argument is a literal placeholder.

         WHY THE ENTRY POINT IS AN INTERPRETER AND NOT `bash` (the one place the platforms differ).
         macOS TCC is per-binary and denies a launchd-spawned `bash` the read of a script, and a
         launchd-spawned `git` the read of a repo, under ~/Documents (#427; the control experiment is
         recorded in #1095). The pass's whole job is `git -C <repo>`, so a bash entry point cannot work
         no matter where the script lives. The binary that DOES hold the access is a checkout's own
         interpreter (PYTHON_BIN below), so launchd starts that on the FARMED launcher (LAUNCHER
         below), which reads the pass itself and hands it to bash on stdin; bash and the `git` it
         spawns inherit the access from that process. The pass and its flags are UNCHANGED. Only the
         process that opens the file is, and that is the whole platform seam.

         SHIPS REPORT-ONLY (#1678, owner ruling 2026-10-11). The installer renders the mode flag below;
         with no explicit flag it renders the pass's DRY RUN flag, never an armed one. Do NOT "tidy" an
         armed install back to report-only without the owner: the measured incident behind the uv class
         (7.1GB cache, 258MB free, every dispatch refused, #1987) recurs as soon as nothing bounds it.
         Do NOT arm it by editing this file either: the installer re-renders from this template on
         every run, so an arm made here is silently reverted. The arming lives in the INSTALLER's
         invocation and is visible in the installed plist.

         A CHANGED PASS RELOADS THE JOB (#1738). An edit to a scheduled script is inert until the job
         is reloaded: the loaded job keeps the program and environment it was bootstrapped with.
         scripts/install-disk-reaper-schedule.sh stamps the pass's SHA-256 and forces
         bootout+bootstrap when it changes, and `status` prints pass_drift so a stale install is
         visible instead of being inferred from a log.

         BUDGETS ARE PINNED HERE, not inherited from a shell (a launchd job gets neither the shell's
         environment nor its rc files, so an unset variable silently keeps a default measured to make
         the pass a no-op):
           REAP_WT_BUDGET_SECONDS 1800: at the 300s default a pass over 454 worktrees spent the whole
              budget classifying and reported DEFERRED=432 (#1095).
           REAP_WT_GH_TIMEOUT 120: the 20s default had `gh pr list` killed by the watchdog on a loaded
              box (rc=124, fail-closed, two passes in a row).
           CACHE_REAP_MAX_MB 2048: the per-cache bound the uv class enforces (#1987).

         No StandardOutPath/StandardErrorPath: every pass writes its own capped log and its pass record
         under the state dir below; an unbounded launchd-captured log is the same disk sink this job
         exists to bound. -->
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>{{LABEL}}</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>{{PYTHON_BIN}}</string>
        <string>{{LAUNCHER}}</string>
        <string>--script</string>
        <string>{{REAPER}}</string>
        <string>{{MODE_FLAG}}</string>
    </array>
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>{{PATH}}</string>
        <key>DISK_REAPER_STATE_DIR</key>
        <string>{{STATE_DIR}}</string>
        <key>DISK_REAPER_REPOS</key>
        <string>{{REPOS}}</string>
        <key>DISK_REAPER_WT_AGED_DAYS</key>
        <string>{{AGED_DAYS}}</string>
        <key>REAP_WT_BUDGET_SECONDS</key>
        <string>1800</string>
        <key>REAP_WT_GH_TIMEOUT</key>
        <string>120</string>
        <key>CACHE_REAP_MAX_MB</key>
        <string>2048</string>
    </dict>
    <!-- Hourly, the same interval as the cells' OnCalendar=hourly, for the same reason: the sinks grow
         continuously (a lane per dispatch, an env per lane) and the failure is a THRESHOLD, not an age.
         RunAtLoad means a freshly installed job reports immediately instead of after the first hour: a
         box whose only evidence is "scheduled" has not been shown to be reaped at all. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>StartInterval</key>
    <integer>{{INTERVAL}}</integer>
    <!-- A background sweep, never a deadline: it must not contend with a lane for CPU or I/O. -->
    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>
    <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
    <true/>
    <key>Nice</key>
    <integer>10</integer>
</dict>
</plist>
